const biography="Charles Albert Brenay was born on 29 March 1897 in Arnstein, in Ontario’s Parry Sound District, the son of Charles Godfrey Brenay, also known as Charles Gottfried Brunne, and Ida Mae Gooley. His father’s German origins and his mother’s Michigan birth connected the household to both sides of the Great Lakes. The family appears under the surname Brunne in the 1901 Canadian census, which places four-year-old Charles in Mills Township. Arnstein’s lakes, forests and small settlements belonged to a region where logging, farming and difficult overland travel shaped everyday life, within the longstanding homelands of Anishinaabe peoples.\n\nCharles crossed into Michigan as a child. His naturalization papers place his arrival at Ossineke in 1903, when he was six. Northeastern Michigan offered a familiar landscape of forests and inland lakes, but its communities were closely connected to Lake Huron’s ports and lumber markets. Nearby Alpena had grown around sawmills, docks and the timber trade; Hubbard Lake had carried floating logs during the lumbering years. Charles grew up in a large household. His siblings included Lillian, James Elmer, William Arthur, Pearl Mae, Mable Agnes, Norman, Nina Violet Gertrude, Benjamin Edward Harrison, Laura Gladys Ida and Lloyd Robert. By young adulthood he was working as a lumberman at Hubbard Lake.\n\nOn 5 June 1918, Charles registered for the American draft under the name Albert Brenay. He gave his birthplace as Arnstein, Canada, and identified Canada as the nation whose citizen or subject he remained. He entered the Army on 29 August 1918 and served as a private in Company C, 78th Infantry. His service extended across the November armistice and ended with his discharge on 21 January 1919. His later veterans card gave a Saginaw address. After returning to civilian life, he petitioned for American citizenship, recording his Ontario birth, childhood arrival at Ossineke and occupation as a lumberman. The Alpena County court admitted him as a United States citizen on 7 June 1920.\n\nThree months later, on 13 September 1920, Charles married Marian Beulah Skinner in Alpena. Their early married life took them to Saginaw, where their daughter Marion was born in January 1923. The older lumber city was becoming an industrial center, with manufacturing and automobile production joining its established river commerce. Charles and Marian’s thirteen children were Arnold Albert, Marion Beulah, David Alvin, Joseph Heber, Henry, Ruth Melvina, Moroni, Virginia Lee, Esther, Martha, Ammon Leroy, Joan Carol and Isaac. Their growing household would move repeatedly between western farms and towns as Charles sought work, land and a dependable living.\n\nIn 1925, Charles and Marian loaded four small children and their possessions onto an old one-ton Ford truck covered with canvas and headed for Arizona. The journey lasted thirty days. Their route passed through Chicago, Omaha, Cheyenne, Salt Lake City, St. George, Las Vegas and Needles. They crossed the Colorado River by ferry near Parker and reached Mesa on 17 July. Gravel, dirt and rough trails made the trip slow; desert heat added to its difficulty. The family’s westward move came as automobiles were opening new possibilities for travel, before dependable paved highways connected the places they crossed.\n\nCharles used practical skills to support the household: he built homes, repaired machinery, operated shoe shops and tried farming. Near Buckeye, west of Phoenix, the family attempted to make a living on desert land. Illness and losses accompanied those unsettled years. Henry died in 1928, Ruth in infancy in 1929, and Moroni in 1931; David died in 1937 at thirteen. A land trade and promised sawmill work drew the family to Nutrioso, but the work did not materialize and they returned to Mesa. They next tried farming near Blanding, Utah, where limited water undermined the hope of raising their own food. A period in Salt Lake City also ended in a return to Arizona. The Depression sharpened the difficulty of finding steady work while feeding a large family.\n\nIn 1940, repeated attacks of pneumonia in their young son Ammon led a doctor to recommend Arizona’s pine country. A farm trade brought the family to Lakeside in the White Mountains. Its higher elevation, cooler climate and timber economy contrasted with the irrigated desert valley around Mesa. By 1942 Charles and Marian were in Sandpoint, Idaho, where a family photograph shows several children gathered outside their home. Another picture places Charles atop a loaded hay wagon, holding the reins of a team of horses, with children seated behind him. His wartime draft registration was made in Sandpoint on 16 February 1942. Later census entries place the household in Utah, including Sanpete County in 1950.\n\nBy December 1966, Charles and Marian were trying to reduce the financial burden of their homes. In a letter to Marion, Jay and the children, Charles described trading their St. George house, clearing its loan and acquiring a smaller house near the temple. Another property in Washington still carried an $8,400 loan, and they were preparing it for sale. He wrote that they would rather live in a chicken coop than carry such a large debt. They had helped Arnold, who was returning to Las Vegas because work had been scarce. Charles hoped that selling the property would make it easier to visit the children, and he remembered enjoying the family’s music, including young DeEdra’s. Five years later, he addressed another note to Marion and “good old Jay,” writing that he had bought two books and was still learning things he wished he had known earlier.\n\nCharles and Marian maintained close ties with their children as the family spread through Arizona, Utah and beyond. Their fiftieth wedding anniversary portrait, taken in Mesa in 1970, shows them together after decades of marriage. Their granddaughter Drinette remembered visits to a home with fruit trees, dried fruit and ample food stored away. The children listened while their mother and grandmother talked for hours. Charles’s legs troubled him in later life, and he often rested with his feet raised while Marian attended to him. He rode a three-wheel bicycle and allowed two granddaughters to borrow it one summer, warning them not to wreck it. They overturned it while learning to ride, escaped injury and kept their mishap to themselves.\n\nThe homes Charles built remained part of the family’s life. Drinette’s household later bought a Lakeside house he had constructed but never fully finished; they continued to call it Grandpa’s house and remembered its sound structure. Charles died in Phoenix on 3 December 1983, aged eighty-six. His funeral was arranged through Meldrum Mortuary in Mesa, and he was buried in Mesa Cemetery, at 1212 North Center Street, Mesa, Arizona. Marian survived him until 1996. Alongside the formal milestones of immigration, military service and citizenship, the family preserved a more intimate remembrance of Charles through his buildings, photographs, orchard and the visits of his grandchildren.";
person.biography=biography;person.note='Canadian-born lumberman, Army veteran, builder and farmer; husband of Marian Beulah Skinner and father of thirteen children.';
